  The secret to beating NetHack is patience.  That means doing a lot of
tedious stuff to reduce risk.  Once the risk is gone, NetHack is a
boring game.  So preparation means doing boring stuff in order to make
the game even more boring.  No wonder players prefer suicide tactics.

  What's worse is that the game gets harder linearly.  Character
improvement gets harder exponentially.  XL 30 takes 5,000,000 times as
many XP as XL 1.

  The best way to ascend is to minimize risk.  Sometimes I've
accomplished that before going below DL 14.  Really.  XL 30 Wizard with
all spells, SDSM, 3x MR, Hp:800 and Pw:1000.  I learned how to set up a
subgame of character development that's more interesting than dungeon
diving or Ascending.  Other players have done the same.

  But with no risk, Ascension in NetHack is deadly dull.  That's why
players will overdevelop characters, then shelve them.  (Even just
character development is more interesting than a risk free Ascension.)


  Let's face it.  NetHack's got a lot of AD&D in it.  AD&D was designed
so a player could run single character for a lifetime, in a social
setting.  The game was designed for interaction between players, not
playing solo.  Look at bones files.  Look at spoilers.  Look at Hearse.
Look at how players complain when they think someone else has an
advantage.  (Why should they care in a single-player game?)  Look at how
successful the combination of NAO and #nethack is.  (A pointer to
NAO/#nethack really should be added to the FAQ in the multiplayer
section.  Probably a pointer to Hearse as well.)

  Heck, look at RGRN.  Why is anyone here wrangling over the game if
it's not more fun than playing the game?  Help isn't the reason.  The
spoilers are instantly available on-line 24/7.  Much faster.  How often
does someone post for help, then report death before there are any
responses?  Heck, why is anyone reading this post?

  And the YA(F)AP, YAFM, YASD posts.  As a game, Nethack's not much to
play.  As a topic of conversation, it's unbeatable.

